<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1857 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Jingle Bells</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song about a fast sleigh ride in the snow, written in 1857. It was not a Christmas song at first, but became one.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>James Lord Pierpont</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>James Lord Pierpont</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>American popular song; later Christmas celebrations around the world</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Massachusetts, United States (Boston or Medford; the place is not settled)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 15 September 1857; copyrighted 16 September 1857</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by James Lord Pierpont (published 1857))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>James Lord Pierpont copyrighted the song as &quot;The One Horse Open Sleigh&quot; on 16 September 1857, credited as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Chorus written and composed by J. Pierpont&quot;, and dedicated it to John P. Ordway, organiser of a troupe called Ordway's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolians</a>. It was first performed the day before, on 15 September 1857, at Ordway Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, by the blackface minstrel performer Johnny Pell. It belonged to the then-popular genre of sleighing songs and was possibly intended as a drinking song. Where and when Pierpont wrote it is not known. A plaque at 19 High Street in Medford, Massachusetts, claims that he wrote it there in 1850, at what was then the Simpson Tavern, and local histories said it was inspired by the town's sleigh races; the researcher Kyna Hamill proposes that it was written in Boston before Pierpont moved south in the autumn of 1857. She also argues that, because its words closely resemble lines of many other sleighing songs and Pierpont constantly needed money, he wrote it purely as a business venture.</p><p>By the time it was published Pierpont had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, as organist and music director of the Unitarian Church, where his brother, the Rev. John Pierpont Jr., was minister; in August 1857 he married Eliza Jane Purse, daughter of the mayor of Savannah, and he never returned north. Oliver Ditson and Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> republished the song in 1859 under a new title, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingle-bells">Jingle Bells</a>; or, The One Horse Open Sleigh&quot;. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> before the motor car, horses' harnesses carried straps of bells to avoid collisions, since a sleigh on snow is almost silent, and the rhythm of the tune seems to imitate a trotting horse's bells.</p><p>The song had no original connection with Christmas. It became associated with winter and Christmas in the 1860s and 1870s and appeared in parlour-song and college anthologies in the 1880s. The banjoist Will Lyle recorded it on an Edison cylinder on 30 October 1889, believed to be the first Christmas record; no copy survives. The earliest surviving recording, by the Edison Male Quartette in 1898, is part of a Christmas medley called &quot;Sleigh Ride Party&quot;. The chorus sung today is simpler than the 1857 original, whose descending chords bear some resemblance to Pachelbel's Canon; who changed it, and who changed the words of the first two stanzas and chorus, is not known, but the modern refrain dates back at least to 1898.</p><p>It became a Christmas favourite in the early twentieth century and one of the most performed secular holiday songs in the world. Hit recordings include those of Benny Goodman and His Orchestra (1935), Glenn Miller and His Orchestra (1941), Bing Crosby and the Andrews Sisters (1943, over a million copies sold), Les Paul (1951) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> Locke (2006). On 16 December 1965 the Gemini 6 astronauts Tom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> and Wally Schirra reported a mysterious object to Mission Control as a Christmas prank and then played the tune on a smuggled harmonica and sleigh bells, one of the first songs broadcast from space; Schirra gave the tiny harmonica to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smithsonian">Smithsonian</a> in 1967. Pierpont was voted into the Songwriters Hall of Fame.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A sleighing song: it celebrates winter fun and the speed of a one-horse open sleigh, its rhythm imitating a trotting horse's bells. Nothing in it was written for Christmas.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dashing through the snow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In a one-horse open sleigh</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O'er the fields we go</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Laughing all the way.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Bells on bob-tail ring</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Making spirits bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>What fun it is to ride and sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A sleighing song tonight.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jingle bells, jingle bells</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jingle all the way,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh what fun it is to ride</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In a one-horse open sleigh, O</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jingle bells, jingle bells</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jingle all the way,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh what fun it is to ride</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In a one-horse open sleigh.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A day or two ago</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I thought I'd take a ride</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And soon Miss Fanny Bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Was seated by my side;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The horse was lean and lank</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Misfortune seemed his lot,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We got into a drifted bank</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And there we got upsot.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A day or two ago</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The story I must tell</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I went out on the snow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And on my back I fell;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A gent was riding by</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In a one-horse open sleigh</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He laughed as there I sprawling lie</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But quickly drove away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now the ground is white,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Go it while you're young,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Take the girls tonight</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And sing this sleighing song.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Just get a bob-tailed bay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Two-forty for his speed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then hitch him to an open sleigh</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And crack! You'll take the lead.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: James Lord Pierpont (1857). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Jingle_Bells" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
